\"Lyre Player and Bird Fresco\") \n\nPylos was a significant 
[Mycenaean](/Mycenaean_Civilization/) [Bronze Age](/disambiguation/Bronze_Age/) 
[city](/city/) located in the region of Messenia,  \n [Greece](/greece/). The 
site is situated on the hill of Ano Englianos and during its Late 
[Bronze](/disambiguation/bronze/) Age occupation between c. 1600-1200 BCE it 
covered a maximum area of 200-300,000 square metres (20-30 hectares). Pylos has 
provided the largest collection of [Linear B](/Linear_B_Script/) tablets on the 
mainland and is the best-preserved palatial centre of the [Mycenaean 
Civilization](/Mycenaean_Civilization/). In [mythology](/mythology/), Pylos was 
the home of the son of [Poseidon](/poseidon/); Neleus, and his son Nestor.\n\n##
The [Palace](/disambiguation/palace/) of Nestor\n\nThe Mycenaean palatial 
centres were used as centralised spaces for economic & political activities, as 
well as spaces for specialised [crafts](/crafts/) & redistribution of 
goods.\n\nCentral to the Mycenaean settlement at Pylos is the Palace of Nestor, 
named after the [Greek](/disambiguation/greek/) hero Nestor; known from 
